# Lenovo Yoga Book 9 14IAH10 (83KJ) — accelerometer mount matrices
#
# Both accel IIO devices share the same modalias (platform:HID-SENSOR-200073)
# so hwdb cannot distinguish them — the second hwdb entry overwrites the first
# for both devices.  This rules file matches on DEVPATH instead, which includes
# the stable HID collection index (.12 / .13).
#
# Correct matrix: negate X and Y (-1,0,0; 0,-1,0; 0,0,1).
#
# The display sensor (device0, .12) is physically inverted (mounted upside-down
# in the top screen lid).  In all landscape use cases (laptop open, kickstand
# dual-screen mode), the sensor raw Y reads ≈ -1G.  Without Y negation,
# iio-sensor-proxy sees corrected_y < 0 → "bottom-up", producing wrong rotation.
# Negating Y gives corrected_y ≈ +1G → "normal", which drives the correct
# kscreen rotation (eDP-1 "inverted", eDP-2 "none").
#
# The base sensor (device2, .13) matrix is irrelevant for orientation (iio-sensor-proxy
# uses the lower-numbered device0 as primary) and irrelevant for tent mode
# (yoga9-rotate reads in_accel_y_raw directly, bypassing the mount matrix).
#
# Supersedes /etc/udev/hwdb.d/61-sensor-yogabook9.hwdb (same matrix, kept
# for upstream submission reference).
#
# HID collection indices were .7/.8 on an intermediate kernel 7.1.3 build;
# they reverted to .12/.13 on kernel 7.1.3-100.fc43.

# Both accel IIO devices — same matrix regardless of HID collection index.
# The wildcard before .auto matches any kernel-assigned collection number
# (.7/.8, .12/.13, etc.) so this survives kernel renumbering.
SUBSYSTEM=="iio", DEVPATH=="*HID-SENSOR-200073.*.auto*", \
    ENV{ACCEL_MOUNT_MATRIX}="-1, 0, 0; 0, -1, 0; 0, 0, 1"
